+## How it works
 
 ### One source of truth
 
 Every part of the app reads and writes the same 54-character facelet string, in
-Kociemba's **URFDLB** order, where each character is both a face name and a
-colour (`U`=white, `R`=red, `F`=green, `D`=yellow, `L`=orange, `B`=blue).
-
-The 3D view, the net editor, the validator, and both solvers all consume that
-same string. The renderer is a strict *view*: it never owns cube state. When a
-move is played, the model is updated first and the animation catches up, so the
-two cannot drift apart.
+Kociemba's **URFDLB** order, where each character is both a face and a colour
+(`U`=white, `R`=red, `F`=green, `D`=yellow, `L`=orange, `B`=blue). The 3D view,
+the net editor, the validator and both solvers all use that string. The
+renderer is only a *view*: the model is updated first and the animation catches
+up, and every turn animates in exactly the direction of the move it shows.
+
+### Solving
+
+- **Shortest** — the Kociemba two-phase algorithm, implemented in-tree so it
+  shares the verified cube model. Phase 1 reaches ⟨U, D, R2, L2, F2, B2⟩ using
+  pruning tables over (twist, slice) and (flip, slice); phase 2 finishes using
+  tables over corner, edge and slice permutations. The tables are built once per
+  visit (about 1.5 s); solving then takes milliseconds.
+- **Beginner** — the seven-stage layer-by-layer method: white cross, white
+  corners, middle layer, yellow cross, yellow edges, then position and twist the
+  last corners. The algorithms were found by search (`tools/ll-lab.mjs`) rather
+  than copied from memory, the last-layer stages use an exhaustive breadth-first
+  search so they cannot get stuck, and every step is simulated before it is shown.
+
+Both solvers are checked on thousands of random cubes in the tests.
+
+### The move engine
+
+`cube-model.js` derives its move tables from 3D geometry rather than a
+hand-typed table, and is pinned down three independent ways: internal
+invariants (turns are permutations, X⁴ = identity, centres never move), an
+independent rotation model (`permutation-truth.test.mjs`), and external ground
+truth (`reference.test.mjs`: the 18 facelet strings produced by cubejs 1.3.2,
+plus known group orders — `R U` has order 105, `R U2 D' B D'` has order 1260).
+The rule is uniform: a clockwise turn, as seen from outside, is −90° about that
+face's outward normal.
+
+### Scanning
+
+You show each face once, held so the camera reads the stickers in the same
+order the model numbers them. The nine stickers are previewed live and the face
+is captured automatically once the cube is held still. After all six faces,
+each face's **centre sticker becomes the reference for that colour** — measured
+on your cube, in your light. Every sticker is then matched to a centre with
+exposure removed (each face is lit differently), under the rule every real cube
+obeys: exactly nine of each colour. Solving that as an assignment problem is
+what separates red from orange and white from yellow. If the result is still
+not a possible cube, the cheapest swap of two doubtful stickers that fixes it
+is applied, and doubtful stickers are outlined for you to check.
+
+### Accessibility and design
+
+Keyboard navigation, ARIA labels on every sticker and control, visible focus,
+`prefers-reduced-motion` support, optional letters on stickers for colour-blind
+users, and a stop-spinning button. `tests/layout.test.mjs` audits contrast
+(WCAG AA), tap-target size, text size and overflow across five screen sizes.
+The stylesheet is mobile first (phone base, 720px tablet, 1040px desktop) and
+strictly monochrome — the only colours on the page are the cube's own.
 
 ---
 
-## Design decisions worth knowing about
-
-**The move tables are derived, not typed.** `cube-model.js` computes its move
-permutations from 3D integer geometry (Rodrigues rotations) rather than from a
-hand-written table. That is elegant but it means a single wrong sign can hide
-from every internal check — a cube with the `U` turn inverted is still a
-perfectly self-consistent cube, just not the one in your hands.
-
-That happened during development, so the move engine is now pinned down three
-independent ways in `tests/`:
-
-- **Internal invariants** — turns are permutations, X⁴ = identity, centres never
-  move, and facelet ↔ cubie-state round-trips over hundreds of scrambles.
-- **An independent rotation model** — `permutation-truth.test.mjs` recomputes all
-  54 sticker destinations per face from a textbook Rodrigues axis-angle formula
-  and compares.
-- **External ground truth** — `reference.test.mjs` compares against every one of
-  the 18 facelet strings produced by cubejs 1.3.2, and against known group
-  orders (`R U` has order 105; `R U2 D' B D'` has order 1260, the group maximum).
-  The correct rule, in the end, is uniform: **a clockwise turn as seen from
-  outside is −90° about that face's outward normal** — the two faces I had
-  hand-tuned wrongly (U and L) were the ones that broke it.
-
-**The beginner method's algorithms were discovered, not recalled.** Rather than
-copy `R U R' U R U2 R'` from memory and hope, `tools/ll-lab.mjs` searches for
-them: it builds canonical situations with the verified move engine and asks for
-the shortest sequence that achieves each stage goal. That is where the corner
-insertions (`R U R'`, `F' U' F`, `U F2 R' F2 R`), the middle-layer insertions,
-and the six "pure corner twisters" come from. Every one is re-verified at solve
-time — the solver simulates before it commits, so a wrong algorithm cannot
-produce a wrong solution.
-
-**Last-layer stages are solved by breadth-first search, not heuristics.** Each
-stage's state space, once earlier stages are fixed, is small (27 states for the
-final corner twists, a few thousand for the rest). So instead of a "keep making
-progress" loop — which can stall in a local optimum, and did — each stage runs a
-BFS over verified algorithms restricted to states where earlier stages still
-hold. It is exhaustive, so it cannot get stuck, and it chains steps (push a
-misplaced piece out, then insert) without special-casing.
-
-**The Kociemba solver is implemented in-tree** rather than pulled from npm, so
-that it shares the verified cube model. It is the standard two-phase algorithm:
-phase 1 reaches G1 = ⟨U, D, R2, L2, F2, B2⟩ using pruning tables over
-(twist, slice) and (flip, slice); phase 2 finishes inside G1 using tables over
-(corner permutation, slice permutation) and (edge permutation, slice
-permutation). Tables take about 1.5 s to build and are built once per page load,
-with a progress indicator and yields so the page stays responsive.
-
-**Scanning is automatic and judged against your own centres.** You show each
-face once, held the way the small guide cube shows (the four sides with white
-on top, then white with blue on top, then yellow with green on top — exactly
-the order the cube model numbers its stickers). The nine stickers are previewed
-live, and the face is captured by itself once the cube is held still for a
-moment. After the sixth face, each face's **centre sticker becomes the
-reference for that colour** — measured on your cube, in your light — and every
-sticker is matched to a centre with exposure removed (each face is lit
-differently) under the rule every real cube obeys: each colour exactly nine
-times. Solving that as an assignment problem is what settles red vs orange and
-white vs yellow. If the result is still not a possible cube, the cheapest swap
-of two doubtful stickers that makes it possible is applied, and doubtful
-stickers are outlined on the review screen. `tests/scan.test.mjs` plays a video
-of a real scrambled cube through a fake camera and requires an exact read and a
-working solution; `tests/scan-classify.test.mjs` covers warm, cool, dim and
-uneven light.
-
-**Accessibility.** Full keyboard navigation, ARIA labels on every sticker and
-control, visible focus rings, `prefers-reduced-motion` support (turns complete
-instantly, the idle spin and solved animations stop), optional letters on
-stickers for colour-blind users, a button to stop the cubes spinning, and a light/dark toggle that stays monochrome (it starts from the system
-setting and remembers your choice). On the solve screen, Space plays/pauses
-and the arrow keys step through the moves.
-`tests/layout.test.mjs` audits contrast (WCAG AA), tap-target size, text size and
-horizontal overflow across five viewports in both themes.
-
-**Mobile first.** The stylesheet is written for phones first and layered up
-with `min-width` queries (720px for tablets, 1040px for two-column desktop).
-On a phone the net editor becomes a row of live face thumbnails plus one large
-3×3 face to paint, the main action stays pinned to the bottom of the screen,
-the cheat sheet is a bottom sheet, and every cube view is capped in height so
-there is always page left to scroll with. Safe-area insets are respected on
-notched phones.
-
-**Theme.** Strictly black, white and greys — the only colours anywhere on the
-page are the cube's stickers and the six palette swatches. The 3D cube sits on a
-dark charcoal gradient so it reads as the hero. Headings use a geometric sans;
-move notation uses a monospace face.
