 ## Known limitations
 
-- **Scanning accuracy still depends on lighting.** Camera scanning is the least
-  reliable part. In good, even, white light it is usually right first time; under
-  coloured or very dim light it can misread stickers — most often red/orange.
-  That is why every face shows a live preview, any face can be tapped to scan it
-  again, doubtful stickers are outlined on the review screen, and the review
-  screen re-validates. Glossy stickers that
-  catch a highlight are the hardest case; tilting the cube a few degrees usually
-  fixes it. Manual entry is always available and is exact.
-- **The shortest solver is not guaranteed optimal.** It finds short solutions
-  (typically 21–24 moves, and optimal for the short cases in the tests) but it is
-  not a full optimal solver — proving optimality takes minutes, not
-  milliseconds.
-- **The beginner method is long.** Roughly 90–170 moves versus ~22 for the short
-  solution. That is inherent to layer-by-layer: it trades move count for
-  memorability.
-- **Built for standard 3×3 colour schemes.** Non-standard sticker layouts (for
-  example a cube whose opposite-face pairs differ from the usual) are rejected by
-  validation rather than solved.
-- **Requires WebGL and, for scanning, `getUserMedia`** (so a secure context —
-  `https://` or `localhost`). Without WebGL the page still works but shows no
-  3D cube; without a camera you can still enter colours by hand.
+- **Scanning depends on lighting.** In even, white light it is usually right
+  first time. Very dim or strongly coloured light, and glossy stickers catching
+  a highlight, can cause misreads (most often red/orange). Every face can be
+  rescanned, doubtful stickers are outlined, and manual entry is always exact.
+- **The shortest solution is short, not proven optimal** (typically 20–24
+  moves). Proving optimality takes minutes, not milliseconds.
+- **The beginner method is long** (roughly 90–200 moves). That is the price of
+  steps you can learn and remember.
+- **Standard colour scheme only** (white opposite yellow, green opposite blue,
+  red opposite orange). Other schemes are rejected by validation.
 
